.TH MODBUS-CACHE 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-cache \- Modbus TCP caching proxy for single-client devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-cache
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-cache \-\-dry-run
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmodbus-cache\fR owns the single Modbus TCP client connection a device
allows (Huawei SUN2000 inverters and their SDongle/EMMA radios accept
exactly one) and multiplexes it: a background loop polls the configured
register ranges into an in-memory cache, any number of clients are served
FC3/FC4 reads from the cache, reads outside the map and other function
codes are forwarded live, and writes (FC05/06/15/16/22/23) are forwarded
when explicitly enabled and audited. Every function code crossing the
wire is named in the log and the audit trail (all standard FCs, FC1
through FC43). A synthesized diagnostics block at registers
65000\(en65003 lets clients tell cached data from live data.
.PP
Resilience: when the device dies, cached reads keep serving last-known
values and forwarded reads get exception 0x0B; when the device rejects a
range, the poll status is remembered instead of hammering it; garbage from
either side is answered one line at a time and never fatal. Verified
against the fake inverter, fuzzing and stress suites \-\- not against real
hardware.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-upstream
the inverter Modbus TCP endpoint HOST:PORT (`:5502` = 127.0.0.1:5502)
(default: config, $UPSTREAM or 127.0.0.1:5502)
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " N"
Modbus unit id (default: config, $UNIT or 1)
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " N"
Modbus connect/read timeout in seconds (default: config, $TIMEOUT or 3)
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " N"
seconds between cache polls (default: config, $INTERVAL or 10)
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-delay " N"
seconds between consecutive inverter reads within a poll cycle, 0 = no
delay (default: config, $READ_DELAY or 0.05)
.TP
.BI \-\-merge\-gap " N"
max register gap when merging ranges into block reads, 0 = read ranges
separately (default: config, $MERGE_GAP or 8)
.TP
.BI \-\-cache\-ttl " N"
per\-register cache TTL: cached registers older than this are refetched
from the inverter, 0 = never expire, serve last known values forever
(default: config, $TTL or 3x interval)
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-write
forward write requests (FC05/06/15/16) to the inverter; default: refuse
them with a Modbus illegal\-function exception (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-write\-registers
register ranges a (forwarded) write may touch: comma\-separated
START:COUNT, e.g. 47400:100,47600:100; "any" = unrestricted; "none" = no
register writable (default: config, $WRITE_REGISTERS or none)
.TP
.B \-\-profile
device profile JSON (the shared profiles/ schema) (default: config,
$PROFILE or the bundled sun2000 profile)
.TP
.B \-\-selection
selection JSON: {"registers": ["pv\-power", ...]} — the profile names to
fetch (default: config, $SELECTION or every profile entry)
.TP
.B \-\-connect\-acl
clients allowed to connect: "any", "none" (refuse everyone) or
comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like 10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16 (default:
config, $CONNECT_ACL or any)
.TP
.B \-\-write\-acl
clients allowed to write (requires \-\-allow\-write): "any", "none"
(refuse everyone) or comma\-separated IPs/prefixes (default: config,
$WRITE_ACL or none)
.TP
.B \-\-listen
listen address HOST:PORT (default: config, $LISTEN or 127.0.0.1:15502)
.TP
.B \-\-local\-time
log timestamps in the system time zone instead of UTC (default: off)
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
refuse further connections past this many simultaneous clients, 0 = no
limit (default: config, $MAX_CLIENTS or 64)
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " N"
disconnect clients silent for this many seconds, 0 = never (default:
config, $CLIENT_TIMEOUT or 300)
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
refuse further connections from one peer IP past this many simultaneous
connections, 0 = no per\-IP limit (default: config, $MAX_CONN_PER_IP or
0)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-port
control server target: unix socket path or TCP port on 127.0.0.1
(default: config, $CTL or /tmp/modbus\-cache\-<listen\-port>.socket); 0
= off
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token": ...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer
(root, the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED)
skips the token; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...) (default: config, $CONTROL_TOKEN or none)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token\-file
the token from a file (the first line; e.g. a systemd LoadCredential or
a key file; must be owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of
\-\-control\-token; giving both is an error (default: config,
$CONTROL_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run the show/clear verbs only (no set/unset, no request dump/restore, no
restart, no shutdown) — the ops shift against the admin key, both
audited; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...) (default: config, $CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN or none)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token\-file
the read\-only ops token from a file (the first line; must be
owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of \-\-control\-ops\-token; giving
both is an error (default: config, $CONTROL_OPS_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-verbs
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated (default: show,clear; validated against the control
grammar at start)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-trust\-uids
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted (default: config,
$CONTROL_TRUST_UIDS or none)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the default 0600) (default: config, $CONTROL_SOCKET_GROUP) (default:
off)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-server
serve the cache's own Prometheus /metrics endpoint (the live `set
metrics enable` toggle exists too) (default: config, $METRICS_SERVER or
off) (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-listen
the metrics bind address HOST:PORT — 0.0.0.0:PORT serves every
interface, a token is mandatory regardless (default: config,
$METRICS_LISTEN or 127.0.0.1:9252)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the /metrics endpoint (the same
scheme the scraper speaks); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first
line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token\-file
the metrics bearer token from a file (the first line; must be
owner\-only 0600) — takes the place of \-\-metrics\-token; giving both
is an error (env METRICS_TOKEN_FILE)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients on the /metrics endpoint; extras get an
immediate 503, 0 = unlimited (default: config, $METRICS_MAX_CLIENTS or
64)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle /metrics client is kept before the socket times out, 0 =
the built\-in 5 s cap (default: config, $METRICS_CLIENT_TIMEOUT or 5)
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent /metrics connections from one peer IP, 0 = no per\-IP
limit (default: config, $METRICS_MAX_CONN_PER_IP or 0)
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-log
CACHE audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald
way); default: config, $AUDIT_LOG or
~/.local/state/modbus\-cache/audit.log — the XDG state home
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " N"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping N backups; 0 = no size
rotation (default: config, $AUDIT_MAX_MB or 10)
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-daily
also rotate the audit log at UTC midnight (default: config,
$AUDIT_DAILY) (default: off)
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " N"
rotated audit backups to keep (default: config, $AUDIT_FILES or 3)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the CACHE audit trail (default: config, $NO_AUDIT_LOG) (default:
off)
.TP
.BI \-\-requests\-audit " N"
audit one REQUESTS tally per client per this many seconds, 0 = off
(default: config, $REQUESTS_AUDIT or 300)
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level
log level (default: config, $LOG_LEVEL or INFO)
.TP
.B \-\-log\-access
log client connects/disconnects at INFO instead of DEBUG (default: off)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-path
the state dump (write\-behind): the live setting overrides and — with
\-\-dump\-with\-cache — the cached words, as one JSON, written 0600 with
an integrity hash (a restore refuses a tampered, unsigned or
group\-writable dump); dumped on shutdown, on the request dump command
and every \-\-dump\-interval seconds; loaded on start only with
\-\-restore\-on\-start, on demand with the request restore command
(default: config, $DUMP_PATH or none)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-key
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
words and the settings ride encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode
keystream, authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and
a configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash; a dump path
without a key warns at start and on set; the value may be `file:<path>`
— the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential
rides as file:/run/credentials/...) (default: config, $DUMP_KEY or none)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-with\-cache
dump the cached words too: `request dump` and the \-\-dump\-interval
loop write the words + the settings (the default off = settings only;
`request dump with\-cache` always includes them; the restore takes
either shape) (default: config, $DUMP_WITH_CACHE or off) (default: off)
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-interval " N"
seconds between the automatic state dumps, 0 = on request (the request
dump command and the shutdown) (default: config, $DUMP_INTERVAL or 0)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the daemon starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — a
restart serves last\-known values instead of cold misses; default: start
cold, load only on demand (default: config, $RESTORE_ON_START) (default:
off)
.TP
.B \-\-config
config file — the set grammar: every line a `set <name> <value>`,
comments start with # (default: $CONFIG, then modbus_cache.conf next to
this script, then the subproject root)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration and register map, print the effective
settings and exit (nothing is bound or connected) (default: off)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
reload the configuration and the register map without dropping clients
(the file always wins over live settings; a broken file keeps the previous
state). The listen address and the log level need a restart.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
clean the cache: all cached values, on-demand entries and poll-status
errors are dropped; the poller repopulates immediately.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown.

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines: one request
\fB{"cmd": "..."}\fR per line, one reply
\fB{"status": "ok|err|bye", "text": "..."}\fR per line. Commands
(the settings registry drives the grammar):
.BR set ", " unset ", " show ", " help ,
the reports
.BR inspect ", " "show status" ", " "show connections" ,
.BR "show cache" " [all]" ", " "show uplink" ", " "show version" ,
the ops
.BR "request dump" ", " "request restore" ", " clear\ cache ,
.BR reload ", " restart ", " shutdown ", " quit .
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B set <setting> <value>
changes a setting live (modbus interval, cache ttl, modbus read-delay,
modbus merge-gap, write allow, write registers, limits max-clients,
limits client-timeout, modbus upstream, modbus unit, metrics enable,
metrics listen, the metrics limits caps, metrics token, requests-audit,
dump-interval, log-level, local-time, modbus profile, modbus selection,
acl connect, acl write, modbus timeout, control port, control token,
control ops-token, control ops-verbs, control trust-uids, control
socket-group, audit log-file, audit rotate max-size, audit disable,
dump path, dump with-cache); a SIGHUP re-applies the config
(the file always wins). A knob the registry marks
.B restart: true
answers the note
.B restart required (startup-only knob)
from set/unset \-\- the config (and a SIGHUP) changes it; show
renders the configured value (a secret knob refuses to show at all
\(em the value renders nowhere).
.IP \(bu 2
.B show cache all
prints every cached entry: name, words, values, age, expiry, poll status.
.IP \(bu 2
.B show uplink
analyses the inverter link: session counters (connects, exchanges, bytes,
errors) and one row per poll block with its status, latency (last/mean/max)
and last error.
.IP \(bu 2
.B request dump
/ \fBrequest restore\fR: the write-behind state (the cached words + stamps,
the live setting overrides) to/from the \fB--dump-path\fR file; the startup
restore and the shutdown dump run the same path automatically. An overridden
secret rides as \fB*** SECRET REMOVED ***\fR \(em the knob renders, the
value never lands in the file, the restore skips the placeholder and names
it. With \fB--dump-key\fR the dump is sealed: the state rides encrypted
(an HMAC\\-SHA256 counter\\-mode keystream, authenticated \(em a restore
refuses a wrong or missing key). The dump
commands \(em and the \fBdump-interval\fR setting \(em show up in the
listings only when a dump path is configured.
.IP \(bu 2
.B clear cache
/ SIGUSR1, \fBrestart\fR (drop the upstream + the cache, repoll),
\fBreload\fR (save the live overrides to a state file and re-exec the
whole daemon \(em the fresh process re-resolves the config, so the
restart-marked knobs take their configured values, and the saved
overrides re-apply once; the cache starts empty),
\fBshutdown\fR (the same as SIGTERM).
.RE
.PP
A second, read-only token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) authenticates
sessions into the show/clear verbs only \(em no set/unset, no request
dump/restore, no reload, no restart, no shutdown \(em the ops shift against the
admin key, both roles audited. A secret knob (control token, the ops
token, the metrics token \(em either spelling) never shows its value:
\fBshow\fR
answers \fBerr <name> is a secret \(em it never shows\fR, \fBunset\fR
refuses \(em pass the secret by flag/env/token-file; \fBshow config\fR
and the dumps render the knob as \fB*** SECRET REMOVED ***\fR (the
config parser refuses the placeholder \(em it is never a value).
.PP
See
.BR modbus-cache-ctl (1)
and
.BR modbus-cache-cli (1)
for the clients.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTL
default control target for the daemon's clients (flag \fB--control-port\fR
wins).
.TP
.B HOST ", " LISTEN ", " ALLOW_WRITE ", " ...
every flag has an environment twin of the same bare name (no tool
prefix); the flag wins over the env, the env over the config script.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/modbus-cache-<port>.socket
the default control socket (stale files replaced, unlinked on shutdown).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/modbus-cache/audit.log
the default audit trail.
.TP
.I cache/modbus_cache.conf ", " cache/selection.json
the packaged sample config and the example selection.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (\fB--dry-run\fR: valid configuration).
.TP
.B 1
invalid configuration (reason on stderr; \fB--dry-run\fR only).
.TP
.B 2
command-line misuse.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-cache-ctl (1),
.BR modbus-cache-cli (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1),
.BR modbus-fake-inverter (1)
.PP
